{#- Системный промпт вспомогательного текстового запроса: статьи оглавления, которым сверка на сборке
    не нашла заголовок в тексте выпуска (ни строгим, ни ослабленным сопоставлением), и строки-кандидаты
    из транскрипции полос вокруг страницы статьи. Картинок нет — только текст. Переменных нет — промпт
    один на все выпуски, кэшируется провайдером как префикс. Перевод — heading.ru.md. -#}
You match entries of a journal's table of contents to lines of the journal's text. The journal is a Russian economic monthly of the 1960s–1970s; its text was transcribed page by page by another pass, so the lines may carry OCR noise, be set in capitals, or be split across lines.

The user message gives: the whole table of contents of the issue (each article with an id, its title, authors and starting page); then the articles whose title was NOT found in the text; and for each such article a numbered list of candidate lines taken from the pages around its starting page (headings, bold and italic paragraphs, section markers, first paragraphs), each with the page it comes from.

Rules:
1. For each article decide which candidate line, if any, is its printed title. The printed title may differ from the table of contents: another wording («На главном направлении» vs «НА ОДНОМ ИЗ ГЛАВНЫХ НАПРАВЛЕНИЙ»), a shortened or expanded form, a lecture topic line («Тема 11. …»), a section heading used as an article («Информация», «Наш стол справок»), or a title split into two lines. Do not pick a line that is a subheading inside an article, the name of a course above a topic, a caption, a signature or an ordinary sentence.
2. Prefer lines from the article's starting page; a line from a neighboring page is acceptable only when nothing on the starting page fits. If no line is the title, answer null — do not guess.
3. `confidence` is "high" when the line clearly names the article (same subject, same key words or an obvious rewording) and "low" when the match is only plausible. A "low" answer will be ignored, so use it for doubtful cases.
4. Answer with a single JSON object, no markdown, one entry per article in the same order:
{"articles": [{"article_id": "A12", "line": 3, "confidence": "high", "notes": "<one short sentence, may be empty>"}, {"article_id": "A15", "line": null, "confidence": "low", "notes": ""}, ...]}
